[[Hal Jordan (DC Universe)|Hal Jordan]] and [[John Stewart (DC Universe)|John Stewart]], two Green Lanterns (intergalactic heroes who wear [[Power ring (DC Comics)|rings]] that give them extraordinary powers), investigate a mystery on Earth.<ref name="DCUSlateVariety" />

Gunn confirmed in December 2022 that the Green Lantern characters would have a significant role in the new DCU.<ref name="GunnDec2022Important" /> When he and Safran unveiled their initial DCU slate a month later, they included ''Lanterns'', a new iteration of a long-in-development Green Lantern series. Safran said the series would be an Earth-based [[Detective fiction|detective story]] and "a huge HBO-quality event" in the style of the series ''[[True Detective]]'' (2014–present).<ref name="DCUSlateVariety" /> Chris Mundy, Tom King, and [[Damon Lindelof]] wrote the pilot script and series bible for ''Lanterns'' by the end of May 2024.<ref name="LanternsGunnMay2024" /> Mundy serves as showrunner for the eight-episode [[HBO]] series.<ref name="LanternsHBO" /> In October 2024, [[Kyle Chandler]] and [[Aaron Pierre (actor)|Aaron Pierre]] were cast to respectively portray Hal Jordan and John Stewart,<ref name="ChandlerLanterns" /><ref name="PierreLanterns" /> while [[James Hawes]] was hired to direct the first two episodes and executive produce.<ref name="HawesLanterns" /> Filming lasted from mid-February to late July 2025 in Los Angeles.<ref name="LanternsLAFilming" /><ref name="LanternsFilmingStart" /><ref name="LanternsFilmingEnd" /> ''Lanterns'' aired from August 16 to October 4, 2026.<ref name="LanternsReleaseSchedule" /> A potential second season was in development by August 2026 and was awaiting a formal greenlight.<ref name="MundyLanternsS2" />

The majority of ''Lanterns'' is set in 2016 and 2026, with the events of ''Superman'' occurring between these two settings.<ref name="LanternsTimeline" /> In 2023, Safran said the mystery Jordan and Stewart investigate in the series leads into the main DCU storyline,<ref name="DCUSlateVariety" /> and Gunn said it would touch upon the story points connecting to Checkmate and Salvation.<ref name="CheckmateSalvationOriginalPlans" /> Nathan Fillion reprises his role as the Green Lantern Guy Gardner from previous DCU media.<ref name="FillionLanterns" />

==== ''The People v. Gorilla Grodd'' ====
{{Main|The People v. Gorilla Grodd{{!}}''The People v. Gorilla Grodd''}}
[[File:Skyler Gisondo as Jimmy Olsen (53817355126) (cropped).jpg|thumb|left|upright=0.75|alt=Picture of Jimmy Olsen actor Skyler Gisondo during filming of ''Superman'' in June 2024|[[Skyler Gisondo]] headlines ''The People v. Gorilla Grodd'' as [[Jimmy Olsen (DC Universe)|Jimmy Olsen]]]]
''The People v. Gorilla Grodd'' is presented as a [[true crime]] [[docuseries]] following host [[Jimmy Olsen (DC Universe)|Jimmy Olsen]] and other ''[[Daily Planet]]'' reporters investigating whether the superintelligent ape [[Gorilla Grodd (DC Universe)|Gorilla Grodd]] was wrongfully convicted of murdering his father, the king of [[Gorilla City]].<ref name="TPvGGOrder" />

In July 2025, Gunn was reported to be considering spin-off television series focused on supporting characters from ''Superman'', such as [[Skyler Gisondo]]'s Jimmy Olsen.<ref name="WSJJuly2025" /> A [[mockumentary]] comedy series featuring Olsen investigating cases involving supervillains, such as Gorilla Grodd, was announced in November, with [[Dan Perrault]] and Tony Yacenda attached as the writers, executive producers, and showrunners, along with Gisondo reprising his role.<ref name="PerraultYacendaOlsenSeries" /> [[Jimmy Tatro]] was cast to co-star as Grodd in July 2026,<ref name="TatroGrodd" /> followed by [[Mary Holland]] joining early the next month as the series's female lead, Sandra.<ref name="HollandOlsenSeries" /> Later in August, HBO Max ordered ''The People v. Gorilla Grodd'' for eight half-hour-long episodes directed by Yacenda.<ref name="TPvGGOrder" /> Filming took place from September to October 2026 in Atlanta.<ref name="PeopleVGroddFilmingStart" /><ref name="PeopleVGroddFilmingWrap" /> The series is set to be released after ''Man of Tomorrow'' later in 2027.<ref name="TPvGGTimeline" />

''The People v. Gorilla Grodd'' is primarily set after the events of ''Man of Tomorrow'', with various other time periods also depicted.<ref name="TPvGGTimeline" /> Additional supporting cast members from ''Superman'' reprising their roles in the series are [[Wendell Pierce]] as the ''Daily Planet'' editor-in-chief [[Perry White (DC Universe)|Perry White]], [[Beck Bennett]] as ''Daily Planet'' reporter [[Steve Lombard (DC Universe)|Steve Lombard]], and [[Mikaela Hoover]] as ''Daily Planet'' columnist [[Cat Grant (DC Universe)|Cat Grant]].<ref name="TPvGGOrder" />

==== ''Mister Miracle'' ====
This series follows the journey of [[Mister Miracle|Scott Free / Mister Miracle]], an [[escape artist]] and a member of the [[New Gods]], and his warrior wife [[Big Barda]] when their home worlds of [[Apokolips]] and [[New Genesis]] go to war.<ref name="MMAnimatedSeries" />

In June 2025, DC Studios and Warner Bros. Animation greenlit an [[adult animated]] DCU series adaptation of the 2017–18 ''[[Mister Miracle#Publication history|Mister Miracle]]'' comic book miniseries written by [[Tom King (writer)|Tom King]] and illustrated by Mitch Gerads. King was attached as [[showrunner]] and an executive producer,<ref name="MMAnimatedSeries" /><ref name="MMDCUConfirm" /> after previously working on [[The New Gods (film)|a planned ''New Gods'' film]] for the DCEU that was shelved in 2021,<ref name="KingNewGodsFilm" /> while Gerads was working on the animation.<ref name="GeradsMMAnimation" /> At the time of the series' announcement, the studios had not yet determined a network for its release.<ref name="MMAnimatedSeries" /> King was nearly finished writing all of the episodes by November 2025.<ref name="KingNov2025" /> In October 2026, [[Josh Hutcherson]] and [[Mackenzie Davis]] were announced to be starring in the series as Scott Free / Mister Miracle and Big Barda, respectively.<ref name="HutchersonDavis" /> ''Mister Miracle'' is expected to be released in 2028.<ref name="MMReleaseDate" />

Nathan Fillion reprises his role as the Green Lantern Guy Gardner from previous DCU media.<ref name="FillionMM" />

==== ''Booster Gold'' ====
[[Booster Gold|Mike Carter / Booster Gold]], a washed-up athlete from the 25th century, travels back in time to the 21st and uses futuristic technology to pose as a superhero.<ref name="DCUSlateVariety" />

When Gunn and Safran unveiled their initial DCU slate in January 2023, they included ''Booster Gold'', an "outright comedy" series set in the DCU.<ref name="DCUSlateVariety" /> A showrunner had expressed interest in taking on the project but had ultimately passed on it by February 2025.<ref name="StatusUpdateFeb2025Variety" /><ref name="StatusUpdateFeb2025THR" /> The series was reported to be "progressing" at HBO Max in July 2025 with [[David Jenkins (television writer)|David Jenkins]] hired to write the pilot and serve as showrunner if the series moved forward;<ref name="BoosterGoldPilot" /> Jenkins took over the series from [[Danny McBride]].<ref name="JenkinsPilotConfirmed" /> Gunn confirmed in April 2026 that the series was still being developed,<ref name="GunnAuthorityApr2026" /> and Jenkins submitted the pilot script that June.<ref name="JenkinsPilotScriptSubmitted" /> However, Jenkins announced the following month that his pilot was no longer moving forward, with DC Studios looking for a new creative team for the series.<ref name="JenkinsNewCreatives" />

